Yes, an event-driven engine can handle real per-component delays.
But I'd put your timing question into a separate static timing
analysis, not the simulator, and keep simulation at unit delay.

Event-driven simulation and real delays

An event-driven engine is the general form of what we have. Each
output change is scheduled for "now + delay" in a time-ordered
queue, and simulated time jumps to the next scheduled change. Unit
delay is the special case where every delay is 1. The quiescence
skip we just built is a crude version of that jump. Scheduling a
change 13 ns ahead costs about the same as scheduling it 1 ns ahead,
so real delays wouldn't make an event-driven engine inherently
slower.

What real delays would cost is in the semantics and the upkeep:

- You'd have to pick a delay model. Real hardware swallows a pulse
shorter than a gate's delay (called inertial delay); a simple queue
passes every pulse through (transport delay). The two give different
answers for glitches, which are the main thing a timing simulation
is for. 
- Typical values can hide races. One number per path shows
one ordering of events. Datasheets give typical and maximum, and a
race between two paths shows up only when you consider the minimum
of one against the maximum of the other. 
- Parity [between the
slow sim and generated C code - Jeff] would force both engines to
change. FR-107 requires the two simulators to agree bit-for-bit,
so real delays in the C engine mean real delays in the debug simulator
too. FR-078 deliberately keeps the debug simulator at unit delay
so you can watch logic settle one level at a time.

What simulation with delays can and can't tell you

The question behind this is usually "is my clock period long enough?"
or "what's the fastest clock this design supports?" Simulation is
a poor tool for that: it measures only the paths the current run
happens to use. Your fib program never exercises the slowest path
through the ALU carry chain with the worst-case microcode word, so
a delay simulation would report success while the real board fails.

Static timing analysis answers it directly. It walks every path
from one register to the next, without any stimulus. For each path
it adds the register's clock-to-output delay, the gate delays along
the path, and the receiving register's setup time, then reports the
longest (critical) path and the fastest clock the design supports.
It's fast and deterministic, needs no change to either simulator,
and it's how the industry splits the job: simulate for function,
analyze statically for timing.

It fits this editor well: a Tools ▸ Timing analysis command that
reports the critical path and its delay, and highlights the path
on the schematic the way the probe or view mode highlights nets.
The complications:

- Memories: the delay from address to data output needs its own
entry. 
- Tri-state buses: the enable and disable times (ten/tdis)
need their own entries. 
- Transparent latches: a latch passes data
through while enabled, so paths through it don't stop at a register
boundary and need special handling. 
- Paths that never actually
happen: the analysis can report a path the logic never lets through,
such as an ALU result the microcode reads a cycle later. That errs
on the safe side; letting you mark such paths as exempt would fix
it.

Why the delay work is stuck, as I read it

The YAML files have the numbers, but which pins each number applies
to is stated only in comments. For example, 74157.yaml has tpd_s:
15 # S -> nY, and 74164.yaml has tsu: 12 # DSA/DSB setup before CP
edge. Nothing can read the pin paths. The key names are also free-form
(tpd, tpd_i, tpd_cp, tsu, ten, …). That blocks timing simulation
and static analysis alike, so it's the real first step.

Suggested order

1. Specify a machine-readable delay format. Each entry names its
kind, the pins it runs from and to (pins or pin groups), and a
value, for example { kind: tpd, from: [S], to: [1Y, 2Y, 3Y, 4Y],
max: 15 }, with the kinds tpd, tco, tsu, th, ten and tdis. Existing
files would be converted, plus a check that flags parts with
incomplete entries. This is small, and needed whichever way you go.
2. Build static timing analysis on top of it. That answers the real
question without touching either simulator.  3. Make the C engine
event-driven for speed, still at unit delay. Build its scheduler
as a general time-ordered queue rather than hard-coding "next step",
so real delays stay possible later without committing to them now.
4. Later, and only if you want glitch or race checking: a real-delay
mode added to both engines together, with the delay model and
typical-versus-maximum question decided first.

